# Description
Adds a service modifier for easy object (de)serialisation as suggested by #272. This follows the earlier introduction of the [Jackson implementation of the `FileManager`](https://github.com/GuusLieben/Selene/pull/275). It expands on this by separating the mapping responsibilities of the [`FileManager`](https://github.com/GuusLieben/Selene/blob/selene-main/selene-persistence/src/main/java/org/dockbox/selene/persistence/FileManager.java) into a new [`ObjectMapper`](https://github.com/GuusLieben/Selene/blob/9342c8697c76b3b0386d867f01e86baf6d344f5a/selene-persistence/src/main/java/org/dockbox/selene/persistence/mapping/ObjectMapper.java) type. The object mapper is responsible purely for the (de)serialisation of objects to either `String` values or files, with additional support for [generic types](https://github.com/GuusLieben/Selene/blob/selene-main/selene-persistence/src/main/java/org/dockbox/selene/persistence/GenericType.java).  

The object mapper is responsible for translating different [`FileType`s](https://github.com/GuusLieben/Selene/blob/selene-main/selene-persistence/src/main/java/org/dockbox/selene/persistence/FileType.java). A precondition for this is that the `FileType` carries a [raw persistence type](https://github.com/GuusLieben/Selene/blob/selene-main/selene-persistence/src/main/java/org/dockbox/selene/persistence/PersistenceType.java).

Support has been added for three different types of (de)serialisation through services:
- `String` targets using parameters
- `Path` targets using parameters
- `Path` targets using annotation metadata

**String targets**
String targets directly call the `ObjectMapper` to (de)serialise a given value. The return type for serialisation should always be `String` (nullable) or `Exceptional<String>` (non-null).
```java
@Service
public interface PersistenceService {

    @Serialise
    String writeToString(PersistentElement element);

    @Deserialise
    PersistentElement readFromString(String raw);
}
```

**Parameter path targets**
Parameter-based `Path` targets look for a given `Path` or `File` (sub)type parameter, indicating the file to read/write from/to. The return type for serialisation should always be `Boolean` (nullable), `boolean` (non-null, false unless indicating otherwise), or `Exceptional<Boolean>` (non-null).
```java
@Service
public interface SampleService {

    @Serialise
    boolean writeToPath(PersistentElement element, Path path);

    @Deserialise
    PersistentElement readFromPath(Path path);
}
```

**Annotation path targets**
Annotation-based `Path` targets automatically look up a given data file from the given owner. The owner can either be specified per (de)serialisation method, or service-wide through the `@Service` annotation. The filename can be provided using the `@File` target, if none is provided the default data file for the owner will be used.
```java
// Uses 'Selene' service-wide
@Service(owner = Selene.class)
public interface SampleService {
    // No specific file was indicated, so the default data file will be used

    @Serialise 
    boolean writeToPath(PersistentElement element);

    @Deserialise
    PersistentElement readFromPath();
}
```
```java
@Service
public interface SampleService {

    // Uses the default data file for 'Selene'
    @Serialise(@File(owner = Selene.class))
    boolean writeToPath(PersistentElement element);

    // Uses the 'sample' data file for 'Selene'
    @Deserialise(@File(owner = Selene.class, file = "sample"))
    PersistentElement readFromPath();
}
```

**File types**
The `ObjectMapper` and `FileManager` both support YAML, JSON, XML, TOML, and Properties files by default. To indicate which `FileType` should be used for a specific (de)serialisation method, this can be added to the respective annotation.
```java
@Service
public interface SampleService {

    @Serialise(filetype = FileType.XML)
    boolean writeToPath(PersistentElement element);

    @Deserialise(filetype = FileType.TOML)
    PersistentElement readFromPath();
}
```

